CLASS:: PhaseLock
SUMMARY:: A bank of phase-locked oscillators driven by an external master phase
CATEGORIES:: UGens>Generators, UGens>Triggers
RELATED:: Classes/BlipSync, Classes/Phasor

DESCRIPTION::

STRONG::n:: phase oscillators, each with its own natural frequency, each pulled
toward an external master phase. The output is STRONG::n:: phase ramps in 0..1
— the same thing link::Classes/BlipSync:: reads with code::freq: 0:: and
code::track: 1::.

The reason the master is a SIGNAL rather than a frequency is that banks can then
be CHAINED. One bank's output phase becomes the master of a bank below it, so a
hierarchy is a tree of these — one instance per node, each with its own coupling
strength — and per-slave ratios let a level lock in whole-number ratios to a
slower one above it. That is the difference from a PLL that generates its own
master internally, which can only ever be one level deep.

Per sample, for slave STRONG::j:::

TELETYPE::
    ph[j] += freq[j]/sr
           + k      * wrap(ratios[j]*master - ph[j])     // toward the parent
           + mutual * R * sin(2pi*(psi - ph[j]))         // toward the siblings
::

code::k:: is a first-order loop, so it captures a detuning of about
code::0.5 * k * sampleRate:: Hz — measured: at 48 kHz, code::k = 0.0002:: locks
groups detuned by 3, 4 and 2 Hz and sits exactly on the boundary at 5 Hz, against
a predicted 4.8 Hz. code::mutual:: is the Kuramoto mean field of the bank itself,
so a bank can cohere internally with no master at all.

NOTE::
Give the slaves different code::iphases::. A bank started with every phase at 0
is a degenerate state: with any coupling at all they simply stay there.
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: ar

ARGUMENT:: master
The master phase, 0..1. A link::Classes/Phasor::, another PhaseLock output, a
link::Classes/BlipSync:: phase output. Audio or control rate; a control-rate
master is interpolated the short way round so the block containing the wrap does
not sweep backwards.

ARGUMENT:: freqs
Array of natural frequencies, one per slave. Its size sets STRONG::n::. Audio or
control rate.

ARGUMENT:: k
Pull toward the master, as the fraction of the phase error applied per sample.
0 leaves the slaves free running. Stable over 0..1 and clipped there; negative
values push away from the master instead.

ARGUMENT:: mutual
Pull toward the bank's own mean field, in the same units. Makes the slaves cohere
with each other as well as with the master; negative repels them into anti-phase.
0 skips the computation entirely.

ARGUMENT:: ratios
Per-slave ratio to the master: slave code::j:: holds code::ratios[j]:: cycles per
master cycle. Wrap-extended to STRONG::n::. INIT RATE.

ARGUMENT:: iphases
Per-slave initial phase in cycles. Wrap-extended to STRONG::n::. INIT RATE.

EXAMPLES::

CODE::
s.boot;

// 1. a bank locked to one conductor, heard through BlipSync
(
{
    var master = Phasor.ar(0, 25 * SampleDur.ir, 0, 1);
    var ph = PhaseLock.ar(master, [53, 71, 105, 123], MouseY.kr(0, 0.001),
                          ratios: [2, 3, 4, 5], iphases: (0..3) / 4);
    Splay.ar(ph.collect { |p|
        BlipSync.ar(0, 6000, phase: p, track: 1, tilt: -2).at(0)
    }) * 0.2
}.play
)

// 2. a two-level hierarchy: four group nodes locked to a conductor, and four
//    members locked to each node. Mouse X is the pull inside a group, mouse Y
//    the pull of the groups toward the conductor. Bottom left = 16 free
//    oscillators, bottom right = four coherent groups drifting against each
//    other, top right = one nested pulse.
(
{
    var g = 4, m = 4, cf = 25;
    var kin = MouseX.kr(0, 0.006), klock = MouseY.kr(0, 0.001);
    var groupF = [53, 71, 105, 123], ratios = [2, 3, 4, 5];
    var master = Phasor.ar(0, cf * SampleDur.ir, 0, 1);
    var gph = PhaseLock.ar(master, groupF, klock, ratios: ratios, iphases: (0..g-1) / g);
    var leaves = Array.fill(g, { |q|
        PhaseLock.ar(gph[q], Array.fill(m, { |j| groupF[q] + (j * 1.3) - 1.95 }),
                     kin, iphases: ((0..m-1) / m) + 0.05)
    }).flatten;
    Splay.ar(leaves.collect { |p|
        BlipSync.ar(0, 6000, phase: p, track: 1, tilt: -2).at(0)
    }) * 0.1
}.play
)

// 3. no master at all: mutual coupling only, which is a Kuramoto bank in
//    phase-correction form rather than frequency-coupling form
(
{
    var ph = PhaseLock.ar(0, Array.fill(8, { |i| 90 + (i * 4.3) }),
                          k: 0, mutual: MouseY.kr(0, 0.01), iphases: (0..7) / 8);
    Splay.ar(ph.collect { |p|
        BlipSync.ar(0, 6000, phase: p, track: 1, tilt: -2).at(0)
    }) * 0.15
}.play
)

// 4. the phase ramps are ordinary signals -- use them for anything
(
{
    var ph = PhaseLock.ar(Phasor.ar(0, 2 * SampleDur.ir, 0, 1),
                          [6, 8.1, 9.7], 0.001, ratios: [3, 4, 5], iphases: [0, 0.3, 0.6]);
    Splay.ar(ph.collect { |p|
        SinOsc.ar(400 + (p * 200)) * (1 - p).squared
    }) * 0.2
}.play
)
::

STRONG::Notes::

LIST::
## A tree of banks is not the same thing as one mean field. In a tree each node
has a definite phase that its children follow, so a group holds together even
while its node drifts; in a mean field a group's phase is only the average of
its members and has no independent existence.
## The correction is proportional to the wrapped phase error, so it saturates at
half a cycle. Beyond the capture range the loop slips cycles rather than
diverging.
## code::mutual:: costs a sine and a cosine per slave per sample; leave it at 0
if the bank only follows a master.
## The bank is allocated at init time, so STRONG::n:: is fixed for the life of
the synth. Large banks need real time memory: raise
code::s.options.memSize:: if the post window reports an RTAlloc failure.
::
